// Operator_Assignment form workflow: On Validate, on CREATE only.
// One assignment record per stage.
//
// Creator does not offer a Unique constraint on a Drop Down field, so the
// one-record-per-stage rule cannot be a field property and has to be a
// validation. Same intent, different place.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS, given getProductionWidgetData unions duplicates rather than
// picking a winner and so copes with two records anyway: the union is there to
// stop bad data being DANGEROUS, this is here to stop it being CONFUSING. An
// admin who wanted to change Cutting's operators and created a second record
// instead ends up with two rows in the report, both looking authoritative,
// neither one describing what the supervisor actually sees. Telling him at the
// moment he does it is the only cheap place to fix that.
//
// Deliberately NOT latest-wins. That would leave the older record sitting in the
// report doing nothing, with nothing on screen to say so.
//
// SCOPED TO CREATE, NOT "CREATE OR EDIT". On edit the record has to exclude
// itself from the check, which needs input.ID, and that is not something to rely
// on unverified after the trouble the client tasks gave. The hole this leaves is
// small and the union closes it safely: editing an existing record's Stage to
// one that is already taken makes a duplicate that merges rather than hides.
// If it ever matters, the edit version is at the bottom.
//
// .count() not .size() - size() is a List method, a query result needs count().

if(Operator_Assignment[Stage == input.Stage].count() > 0)
{
	alert "There is already an assignment for " + input.Stage + ". Open that record and change its operators instead of adding a second one.";
	cancel submit;
}

